# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AISA API — environment.
#
# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) for local development; set the same keys as
# environment variables on your host for production. spring-dotenv loads `.env`
# into the Spring Environment automatically, so `application.yml` reads one set of
# names in both places.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Firestore (the database) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Firebase Console → Project settings → General → Project ID.
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# The service-account JSON. THIS IS A PRIVATE KEY — never commit it, never put it
# in the frontend, and rotate it if it leaks.
#
#   Firebase Console → Project settings → Service accounts
#   → "Generate new private key" → downloads a .json file
#
# Paste it here EITHER as the raw JSON on one line (starting with '{') OR, better,
# base64-encoded. Prefer base64: the raw JSON contains a PEM key full of newlines,
# and most hosting dashboards mangle those in a single-line field — producing an
# "invalid key" error that points nowhere near the cause.
#
#   base64 -w0 service-account.json          # Linux
#   certutil -encodehex -f sa.json out.txt 0x40000001   # Windows
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=

# ── Local development without credentials ───────────────────────────────────
# Set this and the app talks to the Firestore emulator instead, ignoring the two
# variables above entirely. Nothing reaches the real project.
#
#   npx firebase-tools emulators:start --only firestore,auth
#
# Firebase Auth has its own, read by the Admin SDK directly:
# FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099
#
# FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=localhost:8080

# ── Accounts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no JWT signing key: Firebase Auth issues and signs the tokens, and this
# API verifies them with the SAME service account above. One fewer secret to hold.
#
# BEFORE ANY OF THIS WORKS: Firebase Console -> Authentication -> Sign-in method
# -> enable "Email/Password". Nothing else needs turning on; the password-reset and
# verification mails are sent by Google with no configuration.

# Who may sign up. Comma-separated domains; the signup form is public, so without
# this the account list fills with whatever the internet sends it. Set it EMPTY to
# allow any address — a real choice for a college that uses personal mail, but a
# choice, not a default.
AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=bsiet.org

# ── CORS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated exact origins. The frontend is a static site on another origin,
# so the browser will not send a single request that is not listed here.
# Production example:
#   https://yaseenyk.github.io
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3001

# ── First admin ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Created on first boot, and only if no admin exists. Signing up can only ever
# produce a STUDENT, so this is the only way to get the first admin onto a fresh
# deployment.
#
# If an account already exists for this address it is promoted rather than
# recreated, and ADMIN_PASSWORD is ignored — so this cannot be used to take over
# somebody's account by resetting their password.
#
# Set both, deploy once, sign in, change the password, then CLEAR ADMIN_PASSWORD.
# With them unset the app starts normally and logs a warning; the public site is
# unaffected and only the dashboard is unreachable.
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_NAME=AISA Admin

# ── Contact form ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Salt for the hashed caller IP the rate limiter counts against. Defaults to
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT, so there is normally nothing to set here.
# CONTACT_HASH_SALT=
